            <ArticleTitle>Long-term use of a 4% sodium cromoglicate cutaneous emulsion in the treatment of moderate to severe atopic dermatitis in children.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>541-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/09546634.2015.1034077</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Sodium cromoglicate (SCG), a chromone with anti-inflammatory, anti-itch and anti-allergic properties. We report a long-term study of a 4% aqueous solution of SCG in children with moderate to severe atopic dermatitis (AD).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Children aged 1 to 12 years with AD were entered into a 12-week randomised clinical trial (RCT), followed by 12 months open treatment with known 4% SCG emulsion (Altocrom®). Primary endpoint was change in SCORAD score. Secondary endpoints included symptom severity, Quality of Life, concomitant treatment usage, global assessments.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">One hundred and seventy-seven subjects entered, 118 treated with 4% SCG emulsion and 59 with vehicle: 128 completed 12 months in open study. SCORAD score reduced during RCT by -15.3 (-33%) on 4% SCG emulsion and -18.0 (-39%) on vehicle: p = 0.2331. After 12 months reduction was 56%. No secondary endpoint showed differences between treatments during RCT. Thirty-two subjects reported treatment related events during RCT and open trial. Eleven (7%) reported application site discomfort. Most were reported as mild and most resolved without intervention and the study drug was stopped in one case only.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">SCG 4% cutaneous emulsion was well tolerated in children treated for 15 months.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
